\section{Sampling from a formula}{

\code{frm_sample(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)), data = dd, family = gaussian())}
parses, assembles and tapes exactly as \code{\link[frmtmb:frm]{frmtmb::frm()}} does, stops before the
optimizer, and hands the objective to Stan. Every pre-optimizer
refusal still applies (REML, quadrature, the mixture and \code{frmtmb.latent::hmm()}
guards). There is no mode, so the default \code{init} is \code{"random"}:
Stan's own overdispersed initialization on the unconstrained scale,
inside any bounds the prior carries.

The returned object supports the whole draws surface -
\code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:fixef]{frmtmb::fixef()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:VarCorr]{frmtmb::VarCorr()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:ranef]{frmtmb::ranef()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:hypothesis]{frmtmb::hypothesis()}},
\code{\link[=posterior_epred]{posterior_epred()}}, \code{\link[=posterior_predict]{posterior_predict()}}, \code{\link[=posterior_linpred]{posterior_linpred()}},
\code{\link[frmtmb:pp_check]{frmtmb::pp_check()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:as_draws]{frmtmb::as_draws()}} - because those read the model frame and
one draw at a time. The methods that report a maximum-likelihood
quantity refuse instead of inventing one: \code{\link[=check_laplace]{check_laplace()}} (it
compares NUTS against a mode that does not exist here), and on the
embedded object reachable as \code{x$fit}, \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}, \code{\link[=vcov]{vcov()}},
\code{\link[=confint]{confint()}}, \code{\link[=logLik]{logLik()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:fixef]{frmtmb::fixef()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:ranef]{frmtmb::ranef()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:VarCorr]{frmtmb::VarCorr()}},
\code{\link[=predict]{predict()}}, \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, \code{\link[=residuals]{residuals()}} and \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}}.
}

\section{Reparameterization}{

A random-effect block has a FUNNEL in its centered joint posterior:
the width of the prior on \code{b} is a standard deviation that is being
sampled at the same time, so the region NUTS must explore narrows as
that standard deviation shrinks, and one step size cannot fit both
ends of it.

\code{reparameterize = TRUE} (the default) samples \code{z ~ N(0, I)} instead
and computes \verb{b = L(theta) z} on the tape, with \code{L} the block's own
Cholesky factor, which is brms's construction. Each draw is mapped back
through ITS OWN \code{theta}, so the random-effect columns of the draws
matrix
hold the same quantity in the same order under the same names as
\code{reparameterize = FALSE} gives, and every method downstream
(\code{\link[=posterior_epred]{posterior_epred()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:ranef]{frmtmb::ranef()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:log_lik]{frmtmb::log_lik()}},
\code{\link[frmtmb:loo]{frmtmb::loo()}}, \code{\link[frmtmb:conditional_effects]{frmtmb::conditional_effects()}},
\code{\link[frmtmb:hypothesis]{frmtmb::hypothesis()}}) reads them without knowing
which route produced them. Only the \code{stanfit} inside the object
carries \code{z}.

Priors are unaffected. They are declared on the OUTER parameters
(\code{beta}, \code{theta}, \code{betad}), and \code{theta} means the same thing on both
routes; nothing prior-able is reparameterized. What priors DO decide
is which blocks are eligible, below.

\emph{Which blocks, and why not all of them.} A block is non-centered when
two things hold, and they are the same thing twice: every parameter it
has is a standard deviation or a correlation with a Cholesky factor
registered for its structure, and every one of those parameters
carries a PRIOR. Both are about not handing the sampler a direction
it can run away in.
Non-centering gives NUTS the run of \code{theta}'s whole range, and the
centered funnel is what was keeping a chain out of the parts of that
range where the posterior is flat or improper. Removing the funnel
without closing those off first trades a slow chain for a wrong one.

In practice that means a default-prior run, on EITHER route, whose
priors cover every standard deviation and every correlation,
non-centers
\code{(1 | g)} and any block written one term at a time, \code{diag()} and
\code{homdiag()} blocks, \code{\link[mgcv:s]{mgcv::s()}} smooths, \code{equalto()}, \code{gr(cov = )},
and the CORRELATED blocks \code{(x | g)}, \code{cs()}, \code{ar1()} and \code{hetar1()}.
A \verb{k =} Hilbert-space \code{gp()} block non-centers too, since its
factor is diagonal and the defaults prior its sd and its length
scales (brms's classes \code{"sdgp"} and \code{"lscale"}), and so does an exact
\code{gp()} field, as brms's \verb{sdgp * L zgp}: its factor is the Cholesky
factor of the kernel over the field's distinct positions, taken on the
tape at a cost of \code{n^3 / 3} per gradient for \code{n} positions.
\code{rr()} is already non-centered by
construction, since its own coefficients are the standard normal
factors. What stays centered is a run whose variance parameters are
flat: \code{prior = "flat"}, and \code{\link[=check_laplace]{check_laplace()}}, which asks for that
density on purpose.

The call \code{message()}s every block it left centered, with the reason.
A model made only of those samples centered throughout and says so
rather than failing. The reasons:
\itemize{
\item A FLAT PRIOR on the block's standard deviation. Send \code{sd} down with
\code{z} where it is: \verb{b = sd z} goes to zero, the likelihood settles on
the model without that random effect, and the density stops
changing: a flat tail with nothing to stop a chain in it. Measured
on a six-group random-intercept model, one chain of 2000, three
seeds: under flat priors the non-centered chain walks \code{theta} to
-1e15 at a bulk-ESS of 1; under the default \code{student_t(3, 0, s)},
which makes that tail integrable, 174 to 284 against 3 to 48
centered.
\item A FLAT PRIOR on the block's CORRELATION, which is the same
argument: flat on frmtmb's unbounded correlation parameter is
\code{(1 - rho^2)^-3/2}, improper, with all its mass at \verb{|rho| = 1}.
Before 0.39 that kept every correlated block centered, because no
proper correlation prior existed to close the tail; \code{lkj(eta)}
now does, and it is a default, so
\code{(Days | Subject)}, \code{cs()}, \code{ar1()} and \code{hetar1()} non-center
like any other block.
\item A Student-t latent (\code{gr(dist = "student")}): a scale mixture, not a
linear factor.
\item \code{car()}, \code{spde()} and \code{gr(prec = )}: sparse precisions whose factor
is dense.
\item The spatial covariances (\code{ou}, \code{exp}, \code{gau},
\code{mat}): a full factorization per gradient evaluation.
\item \code{toep()}: not positive definite everywhere in its parameterization,
so \verb{b = L z} is not a bijection there.
}

\emph{What it is worth.} One chain of 2000 iterations, three seeds
(dev/benchmarks.md). Where each group's own data say little (the
regime the funnel lives in) it is decisive: 80 groups of 2 binary
observations run at a min-ESS of 236 against 5 centered, 55 times the
effective draws per second. Where the groups are informative it is a
wash: the \code{epilepsy} GLMM and an uncorrelated \code{sleepstudy} are within
noise of the centered chain either way. It is not a blanket speed-up,
and the blocks it declines to touch are the ones where it would have
done harm.

\code{laplace = TRUE} integrates the random effects out, which removes the
funnel by itself, so that route ignores \code{reparameterize} entirely.

The ML fit is untouched either way. The Laplace approximation
integrates \code{b} out, and that integral is invariant under a linear
change of the integrated variable, so \code{frm()} has nothing to change
and no fitted object ever carries a non-centered tape.
}

\section{Laplace draws}{

\code{laplace = TRUE} passes to \code{\link[tmbstan:tmbstan]{tmbstan::tmbstan()}}, which then samples
the outer parameters alone (the coefficients, the distributional
parameters and the covariance parameters) and integrates the rest
out with the Laplace approximation at every step: the group-level
coefficients, the coefficients of a smooth, and the values of an
\code{mi()} term. The draws hold no columns for those. brms has no such
route, because its sampler always visits the group-level
coefficients.

\emph{What the draws answer.} Every summary of the outer parameters:
\code{summary()}, \code{fixef()}, \code{VarCorr()}, \code{posterior_summary()}, the
\verb{as_draws_*()} converters, \code{hypothesis()} on the coefficients and on
\code{sd_} and \code{cor_} quantities, and \code{mcmc_plot()}. Also every predictive
quantity that reads no integrated value: \code{posterior_epred()},
\code{posterior_linpred()} and \code{posterior_predict()} with
\code{re_formula = NA} on a model whose only integrated values are
group-level coefficients, a distributional parameter with no
group-level term, and the default curves of \code{conditional_effects()}.
These are computed from the draws as they are and are exact.

\emph{What they refuse, by name.} A quantity that reads an integrated
value: a prediction at the observed group levels (the default
\code{re_formula}, with or without \code{newdata}), any prediction on a model
with a smooth or an \code{mi()} term that reads them, \code{ranef()},
\code{coef()}, \code{log_lik()} and everything built on it (\code{loo()}, \code{waic()},
\code{psis()}, the \verb{loo_*()} functions, \code{pp_check()}'s \verb{loo_*} types), and
\code{bayes_R2()}. The
refusal names the function you called and says what to do instead;
it suggests \code{re_formula = NA} only where that function takes it and
it computes on the model. Whether a call reads an integrated value is
found out by evaluating it at one draw with those values set to \code{NA}
and to \code{0}: a result that changes read them. A draw whose result has
non-finite values where the first draw's has none is refused the same
way, so a value that vanishes at the first draw only cannot slip
through as NaN.

\emph{Why they refuse rather than fill the values in.} \code{laplace = TRUE}
samples an approximate posterior, and its use is to check that
approximation against draws that sample the group effects. For
predictions, and for anything else that conditions on the group
effects, sample without \code{laplace = TRUE}. Filling the values in would
also be wrong or random: a prediction at each draw's conditional
modes leaves out the uncertainty of the group effects given the
outer parameters, and understated the spread of the in-sample
\code{posterior_epred()} by up to a factor of four on a gaussian model
with 12 groups of 10 rows; drawing them from their Laplace
conditional matched the full draws there, but it would make
\code{posterior_epred()} depend on the random seed and disagree with
\code{log_lik()} and \code{ranef()} about what a draw contains.

\emph{Two edge cases.} On a model with nothing to integrate out,
\code{laplace = TRUE} is the model itself, so the call says so and samples
it on the full route, with draws identical to a call without the
argument. On a REML fit whose objective is sampled as it stands
(\code{prior = "flat"}), the objective integrates the coefficients out
too, and that combination is refused: the draws would hold no
coefficient.
}

\section{Default priors}{

Both routes default to brms 2.23's own weakly-informative
priors, read off \code{brms::default_prior()} on matched models, family by
family. Write \verb{y*} for the response transformed by a link and
\verb{s = max(2.5, round(mad(y*), 1))}:\tabular{lll}{
   class \tab default \tab scale \cr
   \code{b} (slopes) \tab flat \tab - \cr
   \code{Intercept} \tab \verb{student_t(3, round(median(y*), 1) - m, s)} \tab link \cr
   \code{Intercept} (ordinal thresholds) \tab \code{student_t(3, 0, 2.5)} \tab thresholds \cr
   \code{sd} \tab \code{student_t(3, 0, s)} \tab natural sd, log-Jacobian applied \cr
   \code{cor}, \code{rescor}, \code{lncor}, \code{cortime} \tab \code{lkj(1)} \tab correlation matrix \cr
   \code{ar}, \code{ma} (\code{cov = TRUE}), \code{cosy}, \code{lagsar} \tab flat, see below \tab natural \cr
   \code{simo}, \code{sbhaz} \tab \code{dirichlet(1)} \tab the simplex \cr
   \code{theta} (mixture weights) \tab \code{dirichlet(1)} \tab the simplex \cr
   \code{sigma} \tab \code{student_t(3, 0, s)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{shape}, \code{phi} \tab \code{gamma(0.01, 0.01)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{shape} of the negative binomials \tab \code{inv_gamma(0.4, 0.3)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{sigma} of \code{brmsfamily("negbinomial2")}, \code{1 / shape} \tab \code{gamma(0.4, 0.3)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{nu} \tab \code{gamma(2, 0.1)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{kappa} \tab \code{gamma(2, 0.01)}; \code{gamma(0.01, 0.01)} for \code{xbeta()} \tab natural \cr
   \code{beta} (exgaussian) \tab \code{gamma(1, 0.1)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{zi}, \code{hu}, \code{zoi}, \code{coi}, \code{bias}, \code{quantile} \tab \code{beta(1, 1)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{bs} \tab \code{gamma(1, 1)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{ndt} \tab \code{uniform(0, min(y))} \tab natural \cr
   \code{alpha} \tab \code{normal(0, 4)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{xi} \tab \code{normal(0, 2.5)} on \code{tmp_xi} \tab see below \cr
}


The rows marked natural are for a parameter without a formula: the
density is on the parameter itself, with the Jacobian of its link,
which is what brms's bounds do on its constrained parameters. A
parameter with a formula, \code{shape ~ x} or \code{shape ~ 1}, has its default
on its intercept instead, on the link scale, as in brms:
\code{student_t(3, 0, s)} for \code{sigma} (with \verb{y*} on \code{sigma}'s link),
\code{student_t(3, 0, 2.5)} for \code{shape} and \code{phi}, \code{normal(2.7, 0.8)} for
\code{nu}, \code{normal(5.0, 0.8)} for \code{kappa}, \code{normal(1.7, 1.3)} for \code{beta},
\code{logistic(0, 1)} for \code{zi}, \code{hu}, \code{zoi}, \code{coi}, \code{bias}, \code{quantile} and
a mixture weight, \code{normal(-0.6, 1.3)} for \code{bs}, \code{normal(0, 4)} for
\code{xi} and \code{alpha}, and \code{normal(0, 1)} for an ordinal \code{disc}
(\code{lognormal(0, 1)} under its identity link). \code{ndt} with a formula
stays flat, as in brms. A family brms does not have takes the
default of the dpar's name, which is what brms gives a custom family.

For \code{sigma} without a formula \verb{y*} is the response itself (logged for
a log-scale family), whatever the \code{mu} link: brms reads \code{sigma}'s
own link there, which is the identity. \code{com_poisson()}'s dispersion,
frmtmb's \code{nu}, takes brms's \code{shape} default, because it is the same
quantity under brms's name. A mixture component's dpars take the
class's default (\code{sigma2} is class \code{sigma}) and no family's own: brms
asks the mixture, which states none.

The support of each natural default is brms's bound on the class:
frmtmb's link maps onto it (\code{log} onto \verb{(0, Inf)}, \code{logm1} onto
\verb{(1, Inf)} for \code{nu}, \code{logit} onto \verb{(0, 1)}). Two classes differ, and
the default is still the faithful one. \code{ndt} of
\code{\link[frmtmb:shifted_lognormal]{frmtmb::shifted_lognormal()}} is on a log link, and its upper bound,
brms's \code{min_Y}, is the density's: the uniform is \code{NaN} past it, where
the likelihood has no support either. \code{ndt} of frmtmb.eam's
\code{wiener()} is on a link bounded by the fastest response, so the
uniform is flat over exactly brms's interval. A scalar \code{xi} of
\code{\link[frmtmb:gen_extreme_value]{frmtmb::gen_extreme_value()}} is sampled through brms's \code{tmp_xi} (see
the message), and is above -1, where brms's range goes on.

A residual-correlation coefficient that frmtmb holds through a
transform gets brms's \code{(flat)} on the coefficient itself, written as
a uniform density over the whole interval the transform maps onto and
carried inward with its Jacobian: \code{uniform(-1, 1)} on a first-order
\code{cov = TRUE} \code{ar} or \code{ma}, a uniform on frmtmb.spatial's \code{sar()}
interval for \code{lagsar} and \code{errorsar}, and for \code{cosy} a uniform on its
positive-definite window with \code{lb = 0}, which is brms's \verb{[0, 1]}. A
\code{cov = TRUE} term of order two or more, which brms does not have,
takes a uniform on a box that holds its stationary (or invertible)
region, which is flat on that region. A prior of your own on one of
these classes keeps the default's bound on each side it leaves
unset, and a bounds-only one keeps the flat density, as brms keeps
both. The \code{cov = FALSE} coefficients are unconstrained and are the
sampled parameters, so brms's flat needs no density there. A bounded
one of these parameters is sampled through one logistic onto its
interval, as Stan's bound is in brms.

Under a first-order \code{cov = TRUE} \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} or \code{arma()}, the
\code{sigma} that frmtmb reports (in \code{summary()}, \code{sigma()}, \code{VarCorr()}
and these draws) is the MARGINAL sd of a residual, where brms's
\code{sigma} is the innovation sd, smaller by the factor \code{sqrt(1 - ar^2)},
\code{1 / sqrt(1 + ma^2)} or \verb{sqrt((1 - ar^2) / (1 + ma^2 + 2 ar ma))}; on
an AR(1) of 0.9 that is 1.16 here against 0.43 in brms, for the same
model. A prior on class \code{"sigma"}, the default \code{student_t(3, 0, s)} or
one of your own, is read as brms reads it, on the innovation sd, with
the Jacobian of the factor, here and in \code{\link[frmtmb:frm]{frmtmb::frm()}} alike, so a
\code{default_prior(route = "sample")} table passed back as \verb{prior =} is
the default density. A \code{lb} above 0 or a \code{ub} on \code{sigma} there is
refused: a box on the innovation sd is not a box on the marginal sd,
which the sampler holds. Under \code{sigma ~ x} with such a term, a prior on
the intercept of \code{sigma} (\verb{class = "Intercept", dpar = "sigma"}),
the default included, is still read at frmtmb's log marginal sd, where
brms reads it at the log innovation sd; the slopes are the same in
both, as \code{\link[frmtmb:set_prior]{frmtmb::set_prior()}} says.

Every default with a bound (\code{nu} above 1 on an identity link, \code{sigma}
above 0 on one, \code{ndt} below the smallest response, \code{cosy} above 0)
keeps that bound under a prior of your own on the same slot, on each
side the prior leaves unset, as brms keeps the bound it declares.

A mixture's weights without a formula are a simplex, and
\code{dirichlet(1)} on it is the flat density there, with the Jacobian of
the log ratios the sampler works on. No \code{\link[frmtmb:set_prior]{frmtmb::set_prior()}} class
names them (\code{theta} is frmtmb's covariance vector), so the default
is disclosed in the message and \code{prior = "flat"} drops it with the
other defaults.

A \code{mo()} simplex is sampled in softmax coordinates under
\code{dirichlet(1)}, brms's default and the flat density on the simplex,
which \code{prior = "flat"} therefore keeps; the fit's own coordinates
(see "Monotonic effects" in \code{\link[frmtmb:frm]{frmtmb::frm()}}) have folds at the faces
that wall a density off into separate sheets. The draws carry brms's
names, \verb{simo_<term>1[k]}, one column per weight. A \code{simo} row in
\verb{prior =}, \code{set_prior("dirichlet(c(5, 1, 1))", class = "simo", coef = "mox1")}, replaces \code{dirichlet(1)} on that simplex, as in brms,
with the softmax's Jacobian applied.

The \code{disc} default matters more than most: the likelihood cannot
tell an intercept in \code{disc} apart from the scale of the thresholds,
so this prior is what places it.

\code{m} is the mean of the predictor's \code{offset()}, 0 without one. brms
takes it off because an offset moves the intercept by that much, and
only under the links below that transform the response; under any
other link the location stays 0.

The \code{sd} default is written once per response, distributional
parameter and nonlinear parameter that has a random-effect standard
deviation, as brms writes one row for each, with \verb{y*} on the \code{mu}
link. A class \code{"sd"} specification reaches only its own response,
distributional parameter and nonlinear parameter (see
\code{\link[frmtmb:set_prior]{frmtmb::set_prior()}}), so a \code{set_prior(class = "sd")} of your own
replaces the location parameter's default and leaves, for example, a
\code{phi} block's default in place, as in brms.

A multivariate model reads \verb{y*} and \code{s} off each response for that
response's rows, which are written with \code{resp}, as brms's are.

The link is transformed only for \code{identity}, \code{log}, \code{inverse},
\code{sqrt} and \code{1/mu^2} - brms's own list - with a log-scale family
(\code{\link[frmtmb:lognormal]{frmtmb::lognormal()}} and its relatives, whose \code{mu} link is spelled
\code{identity} but whose response is logged inside the density) counted
as \code{log}. Under any other link (\code{logit}, \code{cloglog}, ...), for a
response that was a factor, and for a family whose parameters run
over categories (ordinal, categorical, multinomial), the location is
0 and the scale 2.5 - which is what brms reports for a bernoulli
model. Under a \code{log}, \code{inverse} or \code{1/mu^2} link the ZEROS of the
response are replaced by \code{0.1} before the transform, element by
element, exactly as brms does; the non-zero values are left alone,
so a count response with median 1 keeps \code{log(1) = 0} as its
location.

The call \code{message()}s the priors it chose, one compact line per
class, AND one line per slot it deliberately left flat, so a model
that gets few defaults - or none - says so rather than looking flat
by accident. Wrap it in \code{suppressMessages()} to silence that.
\code{prior_summary()} on the returned draws reproduces the chosen priors
exactly, except the mixture weights' Dirichlet, which no class names.

\emph{What is deliberately NOT matched.} Each of these is named in the
message whenever the model has one.
\itemize{
\item A \code{toep()} correlation stays flat. Its banded parameterization is
not positive definite everywhere, so there is no correlation matrix
over its whole parameter space for an LKJ density to be about.
Every other correlated structure gets \code{lkj(1)}, brms's own default
(see \code{\link[frmtmb:set_prior]{frmtmb::set_prior()}} for what the density is on frmtmb's
parameters).
\item Sum-to-zero ordinal thresholds stay flat: brms puts its density on
thresholds it declares before centering them, a vector frmtmb does
not have.
\item A Student-t group term's degrees of freedom have no prior: frmtmb
fixes them (\code{gr(dist_nu = )}), where brms estimates them under
\code{gamma(2, 0.1)}.
\item NONLINEAR parameters stay flat, which is what brms does: their
coefficients are class \code{b}, and the response's median and mad say
nothing about a rate or a shape sitting inside a nonlinear body.
Write them with \code{set_prior(nlpar = )}, as the brms nonlinear
vignette does.
}

\emph{Overriding and opting out.} A \code{set_prior()} specification takes over
the parameters it reaches. The defaults stay on every other
parameter, as in brms. \code{set_prior(class = "sd", group = "g")}
replaces the default on the standard deviations of \code{g} and keeps it
on the other groups. Adding \code{coef = "x"} narrows the prior to one
standard deviation. A default written per coefficient, such as the
\code{lscale} of each \code{gp()} term, is more specific than a class-wide
specification and stays, as in brms, and so does a more specific
specification of your own. The default-prior message names a
specification that applies to no parameter for either reason.
Write the coef to replace such a default. A named list of prior
objects takes
over exactly the internal parameters it names. \code{prior = "flat"}
turns the defaults off entirely and samples the likelihood, which
warns when the model has variance components: their flat-prior
posteriors need not be proper, and neither the chains nor Rhat can
see that. On a MAP fit \code{"flat"} reaches further than the defaults:
that fit's own prior is taped into its objective, and the bare
likelihood is what \code{"flat"} names, so the penalty is rebuilt out and
the call says which prior it dropped.

\emph{Three sources, most explicit first.} On a fitted model the stack is
this call's \verb{prior =}, then the fit's own prior (\code{frm(prior = )}
makes a MAP fit, and that prior is part of the model the fit is),
then the defaults. Each addressed slot is settled by the most
explicit source that names it, one slot at a time: a call-level
\code{set_prior(class = "sd")} replaces the fit's \code{sd} prior and the \code{sd}
default while the \code{Intercept} default stays. \code{prior_summary()} on
the returned draws prints what the stack came to.
}

\section{Prior draws}{

\code{sample_prior = "yes"} draws from the priors as brms does. Each
prior statement of brms's Stan program for the model is one column
of draws, independent of the likelihood: a prior on a whole class is
one column (\code{prior_b}, \code{prior_sd_g}, \code{prior_sigma}); where a
coefficient has a prior of its own, each coefficient of that vector
is a column (\code{prior_b_x}). A flat prior, the default on class \code{"b"},
has no column. A bounded parameter is drawn from its prior truncated
to the bounds: \code{sigma}, \code{sd} and \code{sds} at 0, \code{nu} at 1, a \code{"b"} prior
at its \code{lb} and \code{ub}. A correlation is one off-diagonal element of an
LKJ draw (\code{prior_cor_g}, \code{prior_rescor}), and a \code{mo()} simplex or a
mixture's weights a Dirichlet draw. \code{prior_Intercept} is drawn from
the prior of the intercept at the centered predictors, which is the
intercept brms puts that prior on, so the population-level
intercept \code{b_Intercept} has no prior draws, as in brms. An ordinal
model with a threshold prior of its own (\code{coef = "1"}) has one
column per threshold, \code{prior_Intercept__1}, ..., as brms does. A
\code{gp()} term's \code{sdgp} and \code{lscale} are not drawn, nor brms's
\code{meanme} and \code{sdme} of a \code{me()} term, which frmtmb has no prior slot
for, nor a GEV's \code{xi} (brms draws no prior of it either).

The draws are made directly from each density with R's random
number generators, as brms makes them in Stan's generated
quantities: they are independent, and a run of the sampler on the
prior would give correlated draws of the same distribution at the
cost of a second run. The saved warmup iterations get prior draws
too, as in brms.

\code{sample_prior = "only"} samples every parameter from its prior with
the likelihood left out (core's \code{prior_only} objective, documented
in \link[frmtmb:frmtmb-sampling-api]{frmtmb::frmtmb-sampling-api}): the group-level coefficients
from their group's distribution, and the latent values of a
\code{mi(sdy)} response from their measurement density, as brms does.
The draws are draws from the prior, with the parameter names of the
model, and the predictive methods give the prior predictive
distribution. Every parameter must have a proper prior: a flat one
is refused with brms's message, which names the parameter as brms's
Stan program does (\code{'b'} with brms's default priors). brms refuses
only a parameter with no prior at all and samples a coefficient
left flat beside others that have a prior; this function refuses
that too. A model with missing values of an \code{mi()} term is refused,
because without the likelihood those values have no density (brms
samples them flat). A model with \code{me()} terms is refused: brms
samples it once its classes \code{"meanme"} and \code{"sdme"} have proper
priors, and frmtmb has no prior slot for those classes yet. Draws
made with \code{"only"} have no prior draws of their own, as in brms.
}

\section{Warmup}{

rstan saves each chain's warmup iterations unless \code{save_warmup = FALSE} is passed, and so does brms, whose fit keeps them in its
stanfit. This function keeps them there too, and the
\code{inc_warmup = TRUE} of \code{\link[=as_draws]{as_draws()}} and its relatives, \code{\link[=as.mcmc]{as.mcmc()}},
\code{\link[=rhat]{rhat()}} and \code{\link[=neff_ratio]{neff_ratio()}} moves them to the draws' names and
scales and puts them before each chain's draws, as brms does.
Everything else reads the post-warmup draws only: \code{\link[=ndraws]{ndraws()}},
\code{summary()}, \code{\link[=variables]{variables()}}, the predictions and the diagnostics.
\code{\link[=nuts_params]{nuts_params()}} and \code{\link[=log_posterior]{log_posterior()}} read the stanfit, and take
bayesplot's \code{inc_warmup}.
}

\section{The pre-flight against the compatibility registry}{

Some models cannot be sampled at all, and the reason usually
belongs to the package that supplies the feature rather than to
this one. Such a package declares the pair through
\code{\link[frmtmb:frmtmb_register_compat]{frmtmb::frmtmb_register_compat()}} with the status \code{"refused"},
and this function reads those rows before it does anything else:
before the rstan and tmbstan namespaces are loaded, before the
formula route calls \code{\link[frmtmb:frm]{frmtmb::frm()}}, and before either route
retapes the objective for its priors. \code{\link[=as_tmbstan]{as_tmbstan()}} runs the same
check. The refusal repeats the registry's own note and names the
row, so \code{frm_compat("frm_sample", "<feature>")} gives the same
answer without running anything.

\emph{What it can decide, and what it will not guess at.} Whether a
model uses a registry feature is settled by WHERE a call sits in
the formula. The check decides three ways.

A response FAMILY is matched by name, which is exact, because a
family is named and never called. It does not look inside
\code{\link[frmtmb:mixture]{frmtmb::mixture()}}, whose family name is the whole string
\code{"mixture(gaussian, gaussian)"}, so a refused \code{gaussian} row does
not reach a mixture of gaussians.

An ADDITION TERM is matched by POSITION. It is written inside the
bar on the left of the response formula and nowhere else, and the
\code{bf()} object carries that, so \code{y | se(v) ~ x} is separated from
\code{y ~ a * se(x)} with a user's own \code{se()}, which frmtmb fits.

Everything else the vocabulary writes as a call is matched BY
NAME, on the display name, outside addition-term position, and
only when a formula actually writes that name and it is not also a
base R function. This is the part that is justifiable from names
alone rather than sound: a name test cannot see that a call sits
where the feature cannot occur, so a refusal registered on \code{mo()},
\code{ma()}, \code{cosy()} or \code{mm()} would also fire on a user's own
function of that name in a nonlinear body. Nothing is registered
on those today. \code{mi_pred()}, \code{gp_pred()} and \code{cs_pred()} are
spelled in the vocabulary differently from the way a formula
writes them, which the registry itself reports, so they warn
rather than matching a call no formula makes.

A refused row naming anything else, a covariance structure, a
fitting mode, another post-fit method, WARNS and samples, and so
does one whose name is a base R function. Refusing would fire on
correct models; silence would make a registered refusal look like
a passed check.

\code{frmtmb.ode} registers the one refused row there is: an
\code{frm_ode()} fit aborts at the first warmup step inside RTMBode,
and the abort leaves rstan's autodiff arena unusable for the rest
of the session, so the refusal is worth more than the attempt.
}

\section{Multiply imputed data}{

Given a \code{frmtmb::frm_multiple()} result, \code{frm_sample()} samples the
fit of every imputation with the same arguments and combines the
draws, as brms's \code{brm_multiple()} combines the chains of its fits:
the draws of imputation 2 follow those of imputation 1, and so on,
and the \code{stanfit} holds every chain, so \code{nchains()} is the number
of imputations times \code{chains}. A \code{seed} gives imputation \code{j} the
seed \code{seed + j - 1}, so the result is reproducible and no two
imputations share a random stream. The draws object keeps the
first imputation's fit, so a method that reads the data in sample
(\code{posterior_predict()} without \code{newdata}, \code{pp_check()}, \code{loo()})
reads the first imputed data set, which is what brms does and warns
about. Chains of different imputations sample different
posteriors, so an R-hat across them measures the imputations as
well as the sampler, as the brms vignette on missing values says;
check each imputation's chains on their own.
}

\section{Multimodal posteriors}{

For \code{\link[frmtmb:mixture]{frmtmb::mixture()}} fits the posterior is multimodal by construction
(label switching at minimum). Mode-centered inits, jittered or
not, leave every chain in one symmetry branch, so Rhat cannot
flag the others; use \code{init = "random"} there and inspect chains
individually.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("tmbstan", quietly = TRUE) &&
    requireNamespace("rstan", quietly = TRUE) &&
    !frmtmb.sample:::tmbstan_build_broken()) {
set.seed(9)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(80), g = factor(rep(1:8, 10)))
dd$y <- rnorm(80, 1 + 0.5 * dd$x + rnorm(8, 0, 0.5)[dd$g], 1)
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd)
ds <- frm_sample(fit, chains = 1, iter = 500, refresh = 0)
summary(ds)
fixef(ds)
hypothesis(ds, "sd_g__Intercept^2 / (sd_g__Intercept^2 + sigma^2) = 0",
           class = NULL)

# the same model sampled straight from the formula, with no ML fit.
# It reports the brms default priors it chose, and prior_summary()
# gives them back.
ds2 <- frm_sample(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)), data = dd, family = gaussian(),
                  chains = 1, iter = 500, refresh = 0)
prior_summary(ds2)
fixef(ds2)

# a set_prior() specification takes over the parameters it reaches
# and leaves the defaults on the rest
ds3 <- frm_sample(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)), data = dd, family = gaussian(),
                  chains = 1, iter = 500, refresh = 0,
                  prior = set_prior("exponential(1)", class = "sd"))
prior_summary(ds3)
}
}
}
